{
  "name": "earnings_drift",
  "ref": "repro:earnings_drift",
  "built": "2026-10-24",
  "claim": "After an outlier earnings move, more than half of companies keep drifting in the same direction over the next month (user seed hypothesis)",
  "tasty_value": null,
  "tasty_sources": [],
  "tasty_note": "User seed hypothesis; no tastylive page or checked episode states a post-earnings drift rate. tastylive's earnings stance is to sell the IV crush and avoid directional earnings bets [page:concepts-earnings].",
  "our_value": "Variant A (|move| > 2x own mean): 51.0% [49.5%, 52.5%], n=4112, mean +0.09% kept drifting at +21 trading days (B: 50.9% [49.5%, 52.2%], n=5507, mean +0.03%; C: 51.0% [50.0%, 52.0%], n=9435, mean -0.03%; all reports: 51.0% [50.4%, 51.6%], n=29122, mean +0.04%).",
  "data": "yfinance earnings dates and adjusted daily closes for 393 current Russell 1000 names (stratified by VONE weight), SPY as the market; zenith data/pead/history.json",
  "period": "report dates 2001-10-15 to 2026-09-22",
  "method": "Two-day market-excess reaction around each report; drift = excess return from the second day's close to +5 / +21 trading days; continues = same sign. Outlier variants A/B/C as in the module docstring. Size = dollar-volume tercile within each year (60 days before the report). 95% CI = normal approximation.",
  "caveats": [
    "Survivorship: today's Russell 1000 members only; names that fell out or were delisted after bad news are missing, which flatters down-move continuation less than it flatters up-move continuation. Treat 2001-2005 as the weakest period (and the smallest: outliers need 6 prior reports, so it effectively starts in 2003).",
    "yfinance earnings dates can be off by a day; the two-day window absorbs most of it.",
    "Events overlap in calendar time (earnings season clustering), so the CIs are too narrow; read differences under ~3 points as noise.",
    "'Size' is dollar-volume within this large-cap universe, not small caps: no Russell 2000 names are sampled.",
    "Excess return vs SPY, not a size/industry-matched benchmark; no costs.",
    "zenith's tracker covers only 2024-05 onward and gates on EPS surprise too, so it is a different selection, not a test of the outlier rule."
  ],
  "verdict": "mixed",
